The latest commit is b23b619, “feat: Docker volume management, persistent DB volumes, and inter-service container network discovery.”
The new commits also introduce AI-driven deployment/task execution, dependency management, test running, service toggles, resource locking, retention pruning, and control-plane/admission/reconciliation work. I’ll review these as implementation changes—not assume the features are complete just because the commit messages describe them—and check whether they address the previous report’s security and reliability findings.
Preliminary review: what improved and what is still unsafe
The new code does make progress: Docker volumes now have ownership labels, services receive network aliases, host-published app ports are bound to loopback, and the Compose configuration no longer publishes Caddy’s admin port publicly. Those are useful changes.
However, I found several serious issues in the latest source:
- Critical — unauthenticated control plane: the API still appears to expose settings, AI actions, deletion, reconciliation, and audit endpoints without visible authentication/authorization middleware. CORS remains origin: true. Combined with the Docker socket mount, this is a major host-compromise risk.
- Critical — host execution remains enabled: Docker-unavailable fallback is still advertised and implemented. The AI deployment executor merges process.env into the application environment, risking exposure of DeployMind’s own secrets to repository-controlled commands.
- Critical — shared PostgreSQL is not safe yet: the admin password remains hardcoded, tenant-provisioning errors can be caught while credentials and tenant records are still returned, and applications share a common Docker network.
- High — Redis tenant isolation is incomplete: ACL creation failures are swallowed, the default Redis user is not disabled in the reviewed setup, and normal application keys will not automatically use the required per-project key prefix.
- High — stop/reconciliation semantics are wrong: a service marked stopped is stopped and removed. That can make the toggle lifecycle unreliable and can break later recovery if the container is not recreated with its original configuration.
- High — retention queries are incorrect: the cleanup code updates/deletes records using id < currentId rather than targeting the specific record. This can affect unrelated records, while errors are swallowed.
- High — volume deletion is forceful: removeVolume() uses forced removal and does not verify ownership itself. A cleanup mistake can destroy persistent data.
- High — approvals are still process-local: pending AI confirmations live in an in-memory Map, so they are lost on restart and are not bound to a durable, immutable deployment-plan hash.
These are code-review findings, not claims that I ran the application or reproduced the failures on a VPS.


